{
  "schema_version": "1.0",
  "kind": "employment_multilingual_pivot",
  "purpose": "Lets people describe their work in their own language. A matched phrase is mapped onto a short Korean pivot term that the Korean lexicon already resolves, and the analyzer then runs its normal Korean pipeline. Before this file, any input outside Korean, English and Chinese returned no candidate at all.",
  "important": [
    "This file contains NO official classification codes and NO legal content.",
    "It only translates everyday work vocabulary into Korean retrieval words.",
    "Every candidate still comes from data/jobcode_master.json through the",
    "analyzer's searchTrack; a pivot term with no official match yields nothing.",
    "",
    "Matching rules (scripts/employment_code_analyzer.mjs, matchMultilingualPivot):",
    " - Latin-script phrases match on whole words, accent-insensitive, so",
    "   'nha hang' (typed without Vietnamese diacritics) still matches 'nhà hàng'.",
    " - A trailing '*' on a Latin phrase allows a word ending to follow, for",
    "   languages that attach endings to the noun (Turkish fabrikada, Uzbek zavodda).",
    " - Non-Latin phrases (Cyrillic, Thai, Japanese, Devanagari, Arabic, Khmer,",
    "   Myanmar, Bengali) match as substrings, because those scripts either have",
    "   no spaces or inflect the word ending.",
    " - The longest phrase wins and its span is consumed, so 'đầu bếp' (cook) is",
    "   never also read as 'bếp' (kitchen), and 'โรงพยาบาล' (hospital) is never",
    "   read as 'พยาบาล' (nurse).",
    " - Words that collide with common words in another language are left out on",
    "   purpose (Vietnamese 'hàn' = weld but also 'Hàn Quốc' = Korea; Indonesian",
    "   'las' = weld but also the Spanish article).",
    "",
    "The 'ko' entries cover Korean colloquial words the base lexicon does not",
    "know (미싱, 가정부, 조선소). The 'en' entries fill English gaps found by",
    "probing ('construction worker' previously returned nothing)."
  ],
